Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 15:55:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] b43: core support for the AC-PHY

Everything the AC-PHY needs from the rest of b43, in one place. Each section
below is one change, with its reasons.

Register a dedicated 5 GHz channel set for the AC-PHY
-----------------------------------------------------

b43_setup_bands() gave every non-N PHY the legacy A-PHY 5 GHz table,
which lists ch 184-216 (5920-6080 MHz). On the AC-PHY those now fall in
cfg80211's 6 GHz grid, where they are not valid 20 MHz centres.

Add a dedicated table with the 802.11ac UNII 20 MHz channels (36-165)
and route the AC-PHY to it. The table is placed before #undef CHAN5G.

Descriptor addresses in the AC-PHY DMA64 index registers
--------------------------------------------------------

The stock driver asks each engine what it takes: it writes all ones to the
ring address register and reads back 0xfffffff0 on the archer-t5e, the
index register keeps 16 bits (0xfff0), and it posts each ring index as the
low word of the descriptor's address (0xbcd58010 on a ring at 0xbcd58000).
brcmsmac's _dma_descriptor_align() asks the same with 0xff0. The older
engines compare only the offset inside their 8 KB-aligned ring, which is
what b43 wrote; an engine that keeps the low address bits gets the right
16 bits from an offset only on a 64 KB-aligned ring, which is why the AC
needed 64 KB rings before. b43 now asks the engine at DMA init and logs
what it reads back; on an engine that keeps the low bits it writes the
address, takes it off the receive status, and writes the ring address
before the enable bit, as the stock driver does.

The ring stays at its 8 KB allocation, with the size in one helper for
alloc and free: the stock driver's AC rings are 32 KB apart.

The engine parameters (outstanding reads, burst, prefetch) stay at their
reset values. The 7.14 stock driver writes TX 0x0370004x and RX 0x0050xxxx,
the 6.30 one other bursts and prefetches, both on x86 hosts; with 7.14's
the DSL-3580L raises a descriptor protocol error (bit 12 of controller 0's
DMA reason) half a second after the MAC comes up, and the controller
restarts in a loop.

Wire AC-PHY into the core TX/RX control path
--------------------------------------------

The AC-PHY (B43_PHYTYPE_AC) is N/HT-like, but the core TX *and* RX paths
never learned about it, so a fully initialised radio transmitted nothing
and every received frame was mishandled.

TX:
 - b43_generate_txhdr()/b43_generate_tx_phy_ctl1(): fill the per-frame
   phy_ctl1 for AC as for N/HT (BW20 / SISO / coding+modulation). For
   legacy 5 GHz OFDM -- the MVP bring-up target -- that mapping matches;
   VHT / 40-80 MHz want a vendor-trace cross-check before being trusted.
 - b43_dummy_transmission(): emit the TXE0_PHYCTL1 (0x1A02) setup for AC.

RX (b43_rx()):
 - b43_rx() reads the PHY type out of the low three bits of the header's
   channel field, which the older microcode fills as (cookie << 3) |
   phytype. B43_PHYTYPE_AC is 0x0b and does not fit in three bits, so on
   AC the frame went down whatever branch those bits selected, or was
   dropped with a warning. What the AC microcode writes in that field is
   not known: the stock driver's cookie in shared memory is a 16-bit
   chanspec, which the old encoding cannot carry either. So on AC the
   PHY type comes from the device, and band and frequency from the
   channel the radio is tuned to, which is also what the OFDM rate
   lookup now takes its band from.
 - signal: where the AC microcode reports the receive power is not known
   either, so AC frames carry RX_FLAG_NO_SIGNAL_VAL rather than a value
   read from the HT fields on the guess that the layout is shared.

Core rate setup:
 - b43_rate_memory_init(): AC joins the OFDM group. It previously fell to
   the default -> B43_WARN_ON and no OFDM/CCK rate memory was programmed.
 - b43_supported_bands(): guard the by-PHY fallback for AC (dual-band).
   The real BCM4352/4360 dev_ids are resolved earlier; this only avoids a
   spurious WARN_ON on the fallback path.

Program the station MAC into shared memory on the AC PHY
--------------------------------------------------------

The OEM driver writes the station address into three shared-memory words
at 0x078c-0x0790. The values are the six address bytes with the low byte
of each pair first, which is the packing b43_macfilter_set() already uses
for the MACFILTER port, and they match the boards' NVRAM macaddr:
00:00:00:00:00:03 on the Netgear D6220 gives 0x0000/0x0000/0x0300 and
00:c0:02:01:07:24 on a BCM4360 board gives 0xc000/0x0102/0x2407. The
routing selector is zero on the capture that carries it, so the
destination is shared memory and not one of the other regions behind the
same object-memory port.

b43 has never touched those cells. It programs the address only through
the MACFILTER port, which is a different mechanism, so this copy is
simply missing.

The write is additive and gated on the AC PHY: it fills cells b43 leaves
alone and does not disturb the MACFILTER path. What the microcode does
with them, and whether the MACFILTER port is still the right one on this
core at all, has not been established; a capture with the address-match
accessors traced will say.

Placed next to the MACFILTER write so the two stay together. The OEM
emits its copy earlier than b43_upload_card_macaddress() runs; b43's core
init has a different order and reconciling the two is separate work.

Program the address match table from core revision 42
-----------------------------------------------------

b43_macfilter_set() writes the MACFILTER port at 0x0420/0x0422, and from
802.11 core revision 42 that is not what the hardware reads. The address match
table has taken its place: 64 rows in the receive-match shared-memory space
b43 already knows as B43_SHM_RCMTA, two words each, with a flags half-word
above the last two address bytes. The top two rows hold what the port used to
-- 0x3f the station address and 0x3e the BSSID -- and 0x8000 in the flags says
the row is live.

So the station address and the BSSID go unprogrammed on those cores. That is
not an omission against a reference trace, it is the receive filter not being
set up.

A cold capture from a BCM43b3, with the OEM driver's writer for that port among
the traced functions, shows it never being entered: the hook is armed and does
not fire once in a full bring-up. The same capture shows the table being used
instead -- all 64 rows written during core init, then row 0x3f with flags
0x8008 and row 0x3e with 0x8002.

The row format is the one keymac_write() has always used for this space, low
address byte first, one word pair per index. The only difference is the second
word: writing it 16 bits wide, as that function did, leaves the flags at
whatever they were, so it is now written 32 bits wide and the flags come from
the caller. Key rows pass B43_AMT_KEEP_FLAGS, which reads the flags back and
puts them in unchanged -- a key row has no business changing what is above its
address.

The row count follows the layout: 64 on the wide one, and the 50 pairwise keys
below it, which is what B43_WARN_ON in keymac_write() now checks against.

42 is the revision the captures report, on all three boards they come from, and
the one on which that writer is never entered. 41 has not been seen and lower
revisions are untested, so they keep the MACFILTER path: getting this boundary
wrong downwards would leave the receive filter unprogrammed, while getting it
wrong upwards only leaves things as they are today. The number is comparable
with what the captures show directly, since the value the driver puts in
B43_SHM_SH_WLCOREREV is this same field.

Write the shared-memory cells the AC cores expect
-------------------------------------------------

A set of shared-memory cells that b43 has been leaving at whatever the
microcode starts them with, and that the OEM driver writes during bring-up.
b43.h already names all of them.

    SLOTT        9        slot time
    MAXBFRAMES   8        maximum frames in a burst
    ANTSWAP      0x000a   antenna swap threshold
    DEFAULTIV    0x000a   default IV location
    RFATT        0x0480   radio attenuation
    HOSTF4       0x0060   host flags 4
    HOSTF5       0x8088   host flags 5
    EDCFQ        0        EDCF queue info
    PSM          0        PSM transmitter address match block

Every value is the same on a BCM4352 and on a BCM4360, across every channel
and both driver versions the captures cover, so none of it is board data.

The slot time is the one value here that is derived rather than observed: 9 is
the 802.11a slot time, the only one that band has. b43_set_slot_time() had been
leaving the cell alone because writing it hurt the transmit rate on BCM4311 and
that was never understood, and returning before doing anything at all on 5 GHz
because the IFS slot register it writes is a 2.4 GHz affair. The cell is now
written from revision 42, which leaves BCM4311 and everything else as it was,
and 5 GHz gets it during core init rather than waiting for mac80211 to report
an ERP slot change that cannot come on a band with one slot time.

For the rest the meaning is not established beyond the name, and the values are
observed. They are written because the hardware is evidently expected to have
them: leaving a cell at a default the OEM driver always overwrites is a guess
too, and the worse one.

Where a cell is written more than once during bring-up -- host flags 4 and 5
gain bits as the driver goes -- the value here is the one it ends at. b43's core init has a different shape from the
OEM's and the intermediate points have no counterpart in it, so the end state
matches and the order does not.

Left alone: the probe response offload cells, PRSSID, PRSSIDLEN and PRTLEN.
b43 turns that offload off with PRMAXTIME, so they are meant to be untouched.

The beacon TSF offset depends on the mode and is written by
b43_adjust_opmode(): both microcode branches start it at 0x183, the band
initvals' value, 7.14 moves it to 0x3a in the BSS setup of an AP (agcombo
and D6220), and the 6.30.223 station keeps 0x183. IBSS, which also beacons,
has no capture and takes the AP value.

Relocate the key index block on ucode42
---------------------------------------

b43.h marks B43_SHM_SH_KEYIDXBLOCK with "(v4 firmware)", and on the microcode
the AC cores load it is somewhere else. Writing it at 0x05D4 corrupts the PHY.

key_write() does two things: an index/algorithm word at KEYIDXBLOCK + kidx*2,
and the key material at dev->ktp + index*B43_SEC_KEYSIZE. The second is
self-locating -- b43_security_init() reads KTP from shared memory first -- and
is correct as it stands. The first is a hardcoded 0x05D4, and b43_clear_keys()
runs it 54 times at attach, from b43_security_init(), before any key exists.
That reaches 0x0640.

On a BCM4352 with ucode42 the block starts at 0x05E0, and 0x05D4 is not key
state at all:

  0x05d4, 0x05da, 0x05dc   the transmit chain mask. 0x3 on a 2x2 board and 0x7
                           on a 3x3 one, which is how they were identified; the
                           AC PHY writes them four times during channel setup.
  0x05d6, 0x05d8           two more cells of the same block.
  0x05e0 + kidx*2          the index block.

A hot capture of a BCM4352 running WPA2 pins both the offset and the encoding.
Two keys are installed, and each write of the index word sits immediately
before the eight words of its key:

  OBJ.WR 0x05e2 = 0x0015    OBJ.WR ktp+0x10 ... (8 words)
  OBJ.WR 0x05e8 = 0x0045    OBJ.WR ktp+0x40 ... (8 words)

0x05e2 is 0x05E0 + 1*2 and the key lands at ktp + 1*16, so kidx 1; 0x05e8 is
0x05E0 + 4*2 and ktp + 4*16, so kidx 4. Which is a group slot and the first
pairwise slot, B43_NR_GROUP_KEYS being 4 -- a GTK and a PTK.

The values say the encoding did not change: (1 << 4) | 5 is 0x15 and
(4 << 4) | 5 is 0x45, so it is still ((kidx << 4) | algorithm), exactly what
key_write() computes. Only the base moved. Algorithm 5 is past the end of the
enum in b43.h, where AES is 3 and WEP104 is 4, so the cipher numbering on this
microcode still needs mapping -- but that is an enum, and it does not affect
where the word goes.

The condition is the microcode identity rather than the PHY type, because the
shared-memory layout belongs to the microcode, and b43 has the precedent:
b43_new_kidx_api() in xmit.h keys off dev->fw.rev for this same family of
cells. b43_request_firmware() already picks "ucode42" for core revision 42 with
an AC PHY, so the test names the same thing the firmware selection does.

ucode40, the other AC entry, has no capture behind it and is left out
deliberately: it will need the same treatment.

The TKIP phase-1 cache at B43_SHM_SH_TKIPTSCTTAK is the other cell b43.h marks
for the v4 layout, and it very likely moved too -- the index block moved by
0x0c and the cache ends where the index block used to start. No capture
exercises it, since it is only reached with modparam_hwtkip, so it is left
alone here.

Fix use-after-free of wldev in b43_bcma_remove
----------------------------------------------

b43_one_core_detach() frees the wldev it is handed, and b43_bcma_remove()
then reads wldev->dev to free the bus device. On a SLUB kernel the freed
object still holds the pointer, so the read goes unnoticed; under
AddressSanitizer it is a heap-use-after-free on the very first remove.

Take the bus device out of wldev before the detach, which is what
b43_ssb_remove() already does.

Let the PHY close a channel switch with its calibrations
--------------------------------------------------------

The AC PHY runs a calibration sequence after every channel switch, and it
has a MAC-state contract the core cannot satisfy from outside: the RX
gain-control sweep that opens it needs the MAC suspended, the calibrations
that follow need it running. On the OEM driver's trace the MAC enable that
b43_op_config() would emit on its way out falls exactly between the two.

Add a channel_calibrate PHY operation that b43_op_config() calls in place of
its final b43_mac_enable() when it has switched channel, with the MAC still
suspended; the hook owns the enable and puts it where its calibration needs
it. PHYs that do not provide it see no change.

Drive the LEDs the board maps on GPIO pins above 3
--------------------------------------------------

b43 knows four LEDs, one per SPROM ledbh0-3 field, and one classdev slot
per role. Broadcom boards map LEDs on any of the sixteen GPIO lines,
through NVRAM ledbh4..ledbh15, which struct ssb_sprom now carries as
gpio_ext[]; on the Netgear D6220 and D-Link DSL-3580L the 5 GHz activity
LED is ledbh10=0x88, gpio 10 active-low, next to the ledbh0-2 defaults on
gpio 0-2. The stock driver builds its LED mask from both -- 0x407 on those
boards -- and drives them from wlc_bmac_hw_up(), wlc_bmac_led() and
wlc_bmac_led_hw_deinit() on the chipcommon GPIO registers; b43 never saw
the pin.

Read gpio_ext[] in leds.c. Pins 0-3 keep their behaviour, defaults
included; a pin above 3 counts only when its ledbh is set, since an unset
field stays at 0 and 0xff is the no-LED value, and an unmapped pin is left
untouched -- gpio 9 may be the ucode's PA control.

One classdev per role could not hold two activity LEDs, so the slots
become a per-pin pool of 2 * B43_MAX_NR_LEDS entries -- an activity LED
takes the TX and the RX trigger on the same pin -- with a flag for the
radio role, which is the one whose initial state follows the radio. The
LED names are unchanged; the LED core disambiguates duplicates itself.

b43_gpio_init() routes the pins of the registered LEDs to the MAC, in both
GPIOCTL and the MAC's GPIO_MASK, next to the fixed 0-3 it always took. The
LEDs are driven the way b43 drives them, through the MAC's GPIO_CONTROL,
not the way the stock driver does; the AC-PHY port does not reproduce the
vendor's chipcommon writes, which the trace comparison keeps in its
perimeter as the core's.

Run the AC-PHY's periodic work every second
-------------------------------------------

The OEM driver's watchdog runs every second -- brcmsmac has
TIMER_INTERVAL_WATCHDOG at 1000 ms, and the captures measure 1.004 s -- and
the AC-PHY's watchdog counts in those turns: a measure block every ten, a
statistics dump every thirty, a noise sample on every one. At fifteen seconds
the CRS thresholds would follow the noise floor fifteen times more slowly.

Add pwork_1sec to the PHY operations. With a PHY that has it, and on the AC
PHY only, the periodic work ticks every second and runs the 15, 30 and 60
second tasks on every fifteenth tick; everything else keeps its fifteen
second tick.

Hand the noise sample to the AC-PHY
-----------------------------------

The AC-PHY requests a background noise sample the way brcmsmac does, with
B43_MACCMD_BGNOISE, and the microcode answers with B43_IRQ_NOISESAMPLE_OK,
which is already in the interrupt mask. handle_irq_noise() only knows the
G-PHY's link-quality sampling; on the AC PHY it now calls the PHY's
noise_sample_done operation instead.

Let mac80211 run the channel availability check on the AC-PHY
-------------------------------------------------------------

mac80211 runs the check. A driver without channel contexts learns about it
from conf->radar_enabled on the config call that tunes the channel, and that
it is over when beaconing is enabled: after the check mac80211 releases the
channel, and start_ap tunes it again with radar detection still on before
enabling the beacon. The core keeps that as a condition, the way
ath10k_recalc_radar_detection() does, in dev->cac_pending: recomputed on every
config call that retunes or asks for radar detection, and cleared by
BSS_CHANGED_BEACON_ENABLED. The AC-PHY reads it when it switches channel and
picks up what the check held back on its next periodic tick.

While the check is pending the station row of the address match table goes
without its flags, so that the BSS does not answer on a channel it may not
use yet.

While radar detection is on, a 150 ms work calls the PHY's radar_poll
operation, the detector read the OEM driver makes at the same period. A pulse
the PHY reports is reported to mac80211 as a radar: the pulses are not matched
against the radar patterns, so this errs towards leaving the channel.
B43_DFS, which depends on CFG80211_CERTIFICATION_ONUS, turns this on.

Make the AC-PHY selectable
--------------------------

B43_PHY_AC depended on BROKEN, and its help said the driver crashes. The
port brings a BCM4352 up as an access point on the DSL-3580L under OpenWrt;
the help now says the support is experimental.

Do not retune the AC-PHY for nothing
------------------------------------

mac80211 passes IEEE80211_CONF_CHANGE_CHANNEL also when only radar_enabled
changes, and around the check it releases the channel -- 20 MHz without HT on
the same primary -- and tunes it again for start_ap. On the AC-PHY a retune
is a full channel setup with its calibrations, so b43_op_config() now skips
the switch, and the calibrations after it, when the configuration the radio
is tuned to has not changed, or when it is that release while nothing
beacons. The tuned configuration is forgotten at core init, where the PHY
init retunes.

Offer radar channels behind CONFIG_B43_DFS
------------------------------------------

cfg80211 refuses the check unless an interface combination declares
radar_detect_widths. ath9k and ath10k declare it only under their
*_DFS_CERTIFIED options, which depend on CFG80211_CERTIFICATION_ONUS; b43
does the same with CONFIG_B43_DFS. Without pattern matching the detector
cannot pass certification, so the option says N.

PHY bandwidth clock
-------------------

The PHY bandwidth field of the core's IOCTL (bits 7:6) follows the
operating width: the stock driver sets it to 80 MHz right behind the
chanspec of an 80 MHz channel (0x155 -> 0x1d5 at the bus on the agcombo)
and its core reset carries it afterwards. b43_phy_bw_clk_set() writes the
field with a read back and keeps it in dev->phy; b43_bcma_phy_reset() puts
it back instead of 20 MHz, which stays the value until a PHY has set it.

TX FIFO geometry
----------------

This section and the next two are MAC-side blocks the OEM driver emits at
core init and b43 did not. They were invisible in the wl-diag captures, which
hook the PHY, radio and shared memory accessors and not raw MMIO; an
mmiotrace of the hybrid wl 6.30.223 on a BCM4360 PCIe card (chip 0x4352
rev 3, 802.11 core rev 42) shows them.

After the initvals and before the first PHY access, wl flushes the TX FIFO
block pool (0x542 = 0x0600, 0x540 = 0x0005, poll bit 0), allocates blocks to
the template FIFO and to FIFOs 0-5 through 0x54a/0x54c/0x520/0x54e/0x550
committed by 0x548, and chains the 42 blocks of the pool: entry i names block
i, block min(i + 2, 41) and a count of min(3, 42 - i), committed by
0x530 = 0x8007 | i << 4. b43 has the register names since the older cores
but never programs them, so the FIFOs keep whatever the hardware was left
with. The values are the same on every bring-up in the trace.

MAC clock fraction
------------------

The initvals set the TSF clock fraction to 0x66662. wl then reads PLL
control words 2 and 3 back from the PMU and writes 2^26 over the MAC clock,
a sixth of the VCO. 7.14 programs PLLCTL3 to 0x100e (VCO 40 MHz *
(24 + 0x100e / 2^24) = 960.0 MHz, MAC 160.0016 MHz) and writes 0x66662;
the 6.30 x86 build leaves it at 0x133333 (963 MHz, 160.5 MHz) and writes
0x6614b. bcma programs 0x100e, so the fraction is 0x66662.
b43_mac_switch_freq() has no branch for these chips; this adds one, with
the fraction as a constant, and calls it from b43_chip_init() for core
revision 42, where the OEM order puts it relative to everything b43 does in
that function.

MAC setup before the PHY
------------------------

On the AC cores the stock driver sets the MAC up right after the FIFO
geometry and before it touches the PHY: operating mode, TSF CFP timings,
interrupt acknowledge and masks, the MAC-PHY clock and the power-up
delay, 1500 us on the BCM4352 and the BCM4360 with both driver versions
captured. b43_chip_init() did it after b43_phy_init(); the block is now
b43_chip_init_mac(), called before the PHY on the AC cores and where it
was on the others, and the port no longer enables the MAC-PHY clock on
its own.

Not in this patch, seen in the same trace: the null-data template wl writes
at template RAM 0x2c (power save), and a GPIO mask write (0x49e = 0) after
each MACCTL write in the sample-collect loop, which the 7.14 reference does
differently. The TX status of this core is in "The AC microcode's header
format" below.

Read back register addresses as the stock driver does
-----------------------------------------------------

The object memory address is read back after every write on the AC
cores: the stock driver does it on the x86 hybrid build and on the MIPS
routers alike (5582 and 4508 reads in the two bus captures).

On BCM63xx routers with a PCIe radio the PHY and radio addresses are read
back too, after every write. That is what flush_writes already does on
the BCM4716; it is now also set there, and b43_write16f() is compiled for
both. With every address write flushed the PCI write counter, which reads
PHY_VER or MACCONTROL every 24 writes, has nothing left to bound, and the
stock driver on those hosts does not do it.

Beacon templates where the AC microcode takes them
--------------------------------------------------

Where the beacon templates go is a constant of the microcode: each loads
the two bases as immediates right before it starts the template engine.
784 (wl 6.30.102.7, and the ucode42 of broadcom-wl 6.30.163.46) takes them
at template RAM 0xd8 and 0x2d8, 832 (6.30.223) and 928 (7.14) at 0x200 and
0x480; the stock drivers write them there. b43 wrote them at 0x68 and
0x468. In front of the frame the AC microcode takes 12 bytes, the PLCP at
byte 3 and the rest zero, and counts them in the template length and in the
TIM position (0x3c + SSID length on 784 and 928); b43 put the 6 byte PLCP
header there.

struct b43_tpl_layout holds the bases, the room for each beacon and the
header, and b43_upload_microcode() picks it by revision; the older cores
get the layout b43 has always used. An AC microcode not in the table gets
none, and b43_op_add_interface() then refuses AP, mesh and IBSS rather than
beacon from the wrong address.

The beacon's PHY TX control word is at shared memory 0xcc on the AC
microcode, not the pre-AC 0x54. The PHY keeps the chain mask in it and the
beacon's power offset in the next word; the encoding in bits 1:0 is the
core's, and b43 left it at the initvals' 0x0044, CCK, which 5 GHz cannot
carry. Both stock drivers set it to OFDM before each beacon template
(0x00c4 to 0x00c5 on the DSL-3580L, 0x0144 to 0x0145 on the agcombo), and
b43 now does the same.

Both stock drivers write MACCMD 0x4 right after the core revision and keep
it in every later MAC command; b43 does the same on the AC microcode.

Template RAM advances its address on each data write, and the stock
driver writes a template with one RAM_CONTROL and then the words (80
words for each beacon on the agcombo, 76 for the probe response). On the
AC cores the template is now written the same way, with MACCONTROL read
once for the byte order instead of before each word. The other cores
keep b43_ram_write().

Not in this patch: the probe response template, which b43 does not
upload at all; the probe requests go to mac80211.

The AC microcode's header format
--------------------------------

b43 tells a microcode's interface by its revision: 598 and above get
B43_FW_HDR_598. The AC cores' microcode is 784.2 in wl 6.30.102.7,
832.127 in wl 6.30.223 and 928.1201 in wl 7.14.43, so it fell into that
format, and the bus captures show two places where it is not that format.

The RX DMA engine: both drivers program RcvOffset 40 (RXCTL 0x036c0851 on
the archer-t5e, 0x00500851 on the agcombo), where b43 programmed, and
pulled, 38.

The TX status: an entry is two packages of four words, read as two passes
over 0x170-0x17c, and the second package has bit 0 set as well (104
entries in the archer-t5e capture). b43 read two words per entry, so it
took the second half for a status with cookie 0, or never left the loop if
the queue only advances on the last word; drain_txstatus_queue() did the
same. The first package is the layout of TX_STATUS40_* in Broadcom's
d11.h, and the 472 statuses of the archer-t5e and MacBookAir6,1 captures
agree with it: the frame ID in the high half of the first word, bit 15 the
acknowledgement, set on the unicast frames and clear on the broadcast probe
requests, bit 2 an intermediate status, bit 3 the PM bit, bits 7:4 the
suppression reason, which is 4, channel mismatch, on three probe requests
of a scan hop that were never sent. Bit 1, which b43 read as the
acknowledgement, is set on all of them. The third and fourth words count
the attempts at four rates, bits 7:0 and 23:16 of each; the frame count is
their sum, 7 on the two frames that reached the short retry limit. The
second package is not decoded.

The TX descriptor: the AC microcode reads the long format of d11actxh_t in
Broadcom's d11.h, 124 bytes, the per-frame fields, four rate blocks and
the link fields, not the 598 header. There b43's cookie, the frame length
and the chanspec are not where it reads them: on the DSL-3580L the first
frame sent over DMA came back with frame ID 0, and the beacon, out until
then, stopped. b43 now writes the long format with one legacy rate: the
frame ID, the length with the FCS, the channel's chanspec, PHY TX control
words with the encoding, the TX cores the PHY keeps in shm 0xcc, the
primary 20 MHz subband and the rate's power offset the PHY writes into the
rate blocks, and the rate's PLCP. The choices the two headers share --
the rates, the MAC flags, the RTS or CTS-to-self -- are made once, in
b43_txhdr_ctl_get(), and each header writes them in its own layout. The PIO scratch space grows to
124 bytes.

The format is B43_FW_HDR_AC and is chosen by the core, core revision 42
with the AC-PHY, the test b43_request_firmware() uses for ucode42: its two
known revisions say nothing about how to order it against the others.
ucode40 has no capture and keeps the revision rule.

The TX header and the layout of the RX header inside its 40 bytes live in
host memory and are not in the captures. Where the AC format shares a case
with 598, every TX header access and the RX header fields, that is the
layout b43 already used on this microcode, not one that has been checked.

Leave the channel cookie to the AC-PHY
--------------------------------------

On the AC microcode B43_SHM_SH_CHAN holds the chanspec, in one layout on
wl 6.30.223 and 7.14 alike: centre channel, position of the primary in the
block, width and band (0x1001 for ch1, 0xd024 for ch36, 0xe29b for ch157
at 80 MHz). The stock driver writes it at the head of the RF bring-up and
at the head of every channel switch, and the AC-PHY writes it in both
places. b43_switch_channel() then read the cell and wrote its own cookie
over it, channel | 0x100, so the microcode was left with a value in the
wrong format. On the AC-PHY the core no longer touches the cell.

The PHY's synth power-up delay on the AC cores
----------------------------------------------

b43_set_synth_pu_delay() wrote 1050 us, the B-PHY value, over the 512 the
AC-PHY writes and both stock drivers end at. The AC cores now get 512. Its
500 for IBSS and idle stays: brcmsmac has no such case, but the stock
driver does, at the start of the 7.14.43 bring-up and on every hop of a
6.30.223 scan, where it alternates 500 and 512.

No hardware crypto on the AC microcode
--------------------------------------

The AC microcode numbers the ciphers its own way: WEP104 is 3 there, where
b43 has 4, and a 16 byte key is 5, past b43's enum; the key fields of its
TX and RX headers are not mapped either. B43_FW_HDR_AC leaves
hwcrypto_enabled off, and mac80211 does the crypto.

Bit 31 of the MAC capabilities on the AC microcode
--------------------------------------------------

Every stock driver in the captures clears bit 31 of the capability register
before it writes the high half to the microcode: 6.30.102.7 with ucode 784,
7.14 with ucode 928 on three boards (0xb0518c05 becomes 0x3051). The one
exception is the x86 hybrid 6.30.223, which leaves it set for its ucode
832, and b43 follows that too: the captures cannot tell the ucode from the
build. What the bit enables is not known.

Bound the power management queue drain
--------------------------------------

handle_irq_pmq() read PS_STATUS until bit 3 cleared, with no bound: a
queue that does not advance on the read would hold the interrupt thread,
and wl->mutex with it, forever. The drain now stops after 256 reads and
warns with the last value read. No capture shows the stock driver
reading the queue, so how many entries it holds and whether a read
advances it are not known.

What leads to a controller restart is logged
--------------------------------------------

The RX descriptor underrun was printed only with CONFIG_B43_DEBUG, and the
TX status anomalies of the DMA rings, the RX ring's poisoned or
unrefillable buffers, the PIO errors and the unknown microcode panic
reason went through b43dbg(). They are printed as warnings or errors, and
a restart dropped because the device is down at info; b43's print helpers
rate-limit them once the device is up.

The cause of a restart, and the "Controller RESET (reason)" line, are
printed through b43err_restart(), outside net_ratelimit(): in a restart
loop the bring-up messages of each cycle use up its budget, and those lines
were the ones dropped. Each fires at most once per restart.

